On Tue, 03 Jan 2006 14:43:50 +0000, Simon Tatham wrote:

> As well as your other point (that a gazillion dollars isn't that much
> better than `only' a bazillion), there's also the point that expectation
> only tells you what you'd expect to happen on average after _arbitrarily
> many_ repetitions of the experiment.

A wand of wishing gives you 5-7 wishes, and if you expect to wish for
several armor items (such as gray dragon scale mail, speed boots,
gauntlets of power, etc.), then you are in fact repeating the experiment
several times as far as AC is concerned, and therefore should aim to
maximize expectation.

The other ways of gaining a wish usually only give you one wish, and in
that case one might aim to maximize utility over expectation.

Helms of brilliance and gauntlets of dexterity are exceptions to the
expectation rule, because these items have effects beyond just AC
reduction, and one cannot accumulate the attribute bonuses over several
wish attempts in the same way that one can accumulate AC. I always wish
for +2 with these items because a +0 item is useless enough to be worth
avoiding, especially early in the game. Sometimes I even wish for a +1
item if I'm right at the boundary where a +1 bonus makes a difference.


